# From a bare Debian 13 install to a calango-nix desktop

Generated by `home/bootstrap.nix`. Every package name, group and command below
comes from `calango.bootstrap.*`, so this file cannot disagree with the flake.

Replace `<user>` with the account name and `<host>` with the machine's
hostname throughout.

## What orders this

Six constraints, and every one of them has cost somebody something:

1. **Nix before everything.** apt supplies `nix-bin`; `nix-daemon` is a root
   service.
2. **The `nix-users` group needs a new login.**
   `/nix/var/nix/daemon-socket/` is `0770 root:nix-users`. Until you log in
   again, prefix each command with `sg nix-users -c '…'`.
3. **The repositories before the corp packages.**
   @corpRepoPackagesCount@ of @corpPackagesCount@ come from a third-party
   repository.
4. **The corp packages before `calango-desktop`.** apt resolves `Depends`
   before it unpacks anything, and @corpFileOnlyCount@ of @corpPackagesCount@
   are reachable from no repository at all.
5. **`calango-desktop` before the first login.** It ships the greetd session
   entry.
6. **The first `activate` before the first login.** The session entry runs
   `$HOME/.nix-profile/bin/uwsm`.

Each stage ends with a **gate**. Run it. Do not go on until it answers as
shown.

---

## Stage A — as root

Whether `sudo` even works yet depends on how this machine was installed: the
Debian installer puts the first user in the `sudo` group only when root was
given an **empty** password. A machine installed with a real root password
leaves the first user out of `sudo` -- and `sudo` is `Priority: optional` and
absent from `calango.bootstrap.packages.base`, so it may not be on the
machine at all yet. `sudo apt install ...` on such a machine fails before it
does anything, with `sudo` reported as an unknown command.

On that kind of machine, become root directly instead of through `sudo`:

```sh
su -
apt install @basePackagesOneLine@
usermod -aG @groupsComma@ <user>
usermod -aG sudo <user>
```

Where the account already has `sudo` -- root was given an empty password, or
someone already ran the block above -- run the same three commands prefixed
with `sudo` from `<user>`'s own shell instead of `su -`. Either way, `<user>`
must end Stage A holding `sudo`: Stage C runs it from `<user>`'s own shell,
not from a root login, and `usermod -aG sudo <user>` is what puts it there.
If you are unsure which case applies, `id -nG <user>` after Gate A below
settles it.

Why each package is there:

@basePackagesTable@

### Gate A

```sh
systemctl is-active nix-daemon.service                 # active
id -nG <user> | tr ' ' '\n' | grep -cx @groupsGrepArgs@   # @groupsCount@
```

Check the **service**, not the socket. `nix-daemon.socket` reads
`inactive (dead)` for ever on a working install, because the service is
`WantedBy=multi-user.target` and binds the socket itself.

The group count is answered by `id -nG <user>`, which reads the account's
groups from the group database. Your own shell does not have them until you
log in again.

---

## Stage B — as `<user>`

```sh
mkdir -p ~/.config/nix
printf 'experimental-features = nix-command flakes\n' > ~/.config/nix/nix.conf
git clone <this repository> ~/Projects/calango-nix
cd ~/Projects/calango-nix
```

Write this machine's hardware facts. Only the first is required:

```sh
cp hypr/hosts/suffer.lua hypr/hosts/<host>.lua      # then edit the outputs
cp foot/hosts/suffer.ini foot/hosts/<host>.ini      # optional
cp gtk/hosts/suffer.conf gtk/hosts/<host>.conf      # optional
```

`hypr/hosts/<host>.lua` is required and the build enforces it. Without it
`primary` is nil and **every workspace rule becomes a no-op** — the 1-5 and
6-10 split, the VSCode workspace, the persistent YouTube workspace. The
desktop starts and looks correct, which is why this is a build error rather
than a runtime warning.

Add the host in `flake.nix`, beside the existing entry:

```nix
      <host> = mkHome "<user>" "<host>";
```

```nix
      hostConfigs = {
        "isutton@suffer" = suffer;
        "<user>@<host>" = <host>;
      };
```

Then build both artifacts:

```sh
sg nix-users -c 'nix flake check'
sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoBootstrap'
sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoDeb'
sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths \
  .#homeConfigurations."<user>@<host>".activationPackage'
```

### Gate B

```sh
sg nix-users -c 'nix flake check' 2>&1 | grep -o 'running [0-9]* flake checks'
```

Count the checks; do not compare against a number written down anywhere. Then:

```sh
B=$(sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoBootstrap')
find "$B" -type f | sort
./test/apt-sources.sh "$B"        # all @aptSourceCount@ source(s) verified
```

---

## Stage C — as `<user>`, elevating with `sudo`

Each stage has to stand on its own: nothing says the same shell survives from
Stage B, and a reader who opens a fresh one here has neither `$B` nor the
repository as a working directory. Re-derive both before anything else:

```sh
cd ~/Projects/calango-nix
B=$(sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoBootstrap')
```

**Install the repositories first.** These files are scaffolding, not state.
They exist so that one `apt install` can succeed; each vendor package then
writes its own copy from its own `postinst`, and Chrome's from
`/etc/cron.daily/google-chrome`.

```sh
sudo install -Dm644 -t /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ "$B"/etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.sources
sudo apt update
```

**Then the corp packages.** @corpRepoPackagesCount@ come from those
repositories:

```sh
sudo apt install @corpRepoPackagesOneLine@
```

**Now delete the scaffolding, before any other apt command.** This is not
tidying and it is not optional. Two of the four vendor packages -- Chrome and
1Password -- write their own source file from their `postinst`, naming a keyring
under `/usr/share/keyrings` where the scaffolding file carries an inline key. apt
treats one repository reached through two different `Signed-By` values as an
**error**, not as a duplicate warning, and it then refuses to read the source
list at all:

```
  -----END PGP PUBLIC KEY BLOCK-----
   != /usr/share/keyrings/google-chrome.gpg
E: The list of sources could not be read.
```

Measured on a bare Debian 13.6 machine: with the scaffolding left in place, the
next two `apt install` commands each returned exit 100 and nothing further in
this stage could run.

```sh
sudo rm -f /etc/apt/sources.list.d/calango-bootstrap-*.sources
sudo apt update
```

The distinct `calango-bootstrap-` prefix is what makes that one command safe --
the vendors name their own files after themselves, so those are left alone.

**Then the @corpFileOnlyCount@ that come from no repository at all.** Fetch
each `.deb` and install it from the file:

```sh
./bin/slack-latest                 # prints the current version and the commands
sudo apt install ./slack-desktop-<version>-amd64.deb
sudo apt install ./fresh-editor_<version>_amd64.deb
```

Where each comes from:

@corpPackagesTable@

**Then the metapackage.** It pulls the Debian-side keeps as its own `Depends`:

```sh
D=$(sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoDeb')
sudo apt install "$D"/calango-desktop_*.deb
```

**And the greetd configuration.** This is the one file here that can leave the
machine unable to reach a desktop, so read Gate C before rebooting:

```sh
sudo install -Dm644 "$B/etc/greetd/config.toml" /etc/greetd/config.toml
sudo systemctl enable greetd.service
```

On a bare machine dpkg asks **nothing** during any of this. On suffer
`/etc/default/slack` already existed unowned, so dpkg raised a conffile prompt
whose default answer re-armed a dead repository. Slack's `.deb` ships no such
file — its cron job creates one — so installing `calango-desktop` on the first
day means there is nothing to prompt about.

### Gate C

```sh
dpkg-query -W -f='${db:Status-Abbrev} ${Package}\n' calango-desktop   # ii calango-desktop
apt-get -s autoremove | grep -c '^Remv '                              # 0
test -f /usr/share/wayland-sessions/hyprland-nix.desktop && echo present
cmp -s /etc/greetd/config.toml "$B/etc/greetd/config.toml" && echo greetd-ok
```

---

## Stage D — as `<user>`

The first switch cannot use `home-manager`, because
`programs.home-manager.enable = true` supplies that command only after a
generation exists. Do **not** use `nix run home-manager`: that reads the flake
registry, which tracks unstable rather than this flake's pinned
`release-26.05`. Use the pinned activation package:

```sh
p=$(sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths \
  .#homeConfigurations."<user>@<host>".activationPackage')
"$p/activate"
```

Then log out and select **Hyprland (Nix)** in tuigreet.

### Gate D

```sh
test -x ~/.nix-profile/bin/uwsm && echo uwsm-present
loginctl show-session "$XDG_SESSION_ID" -p Type --value      # wayland
tr '\0' '\n' < /proc/$(pgrep -x .Hyprland-wrapp)/environ \
  | grep -c LIBGL_DRIVERS_PATH                               # 1
```

The `pgrep` pattern is `.Hyprland-wrapp`, truncated at fifteen characters,
because Nix wraps the binary. `pgrep -x Hyprland` matches nothing in the
working state and the broken state alike.

---

## Stage E — what is deliberately not here

- **The live state.** Fonts, syncthing, Signal, the keyring and `~/.ssh` are
  not migrated. A second machine re-pairs and re-links.
- **The corporate enrolment.** `endpoint-verification` is a managed-device
  agent.
- **A `ufw` rule.** This flake ships the profile vocabulary; `ufw allow
  calango-syncthing` is a human act, because `/etc/ufw/user.rules` is
  `0640 root:root` and nothing unprivileged can verify a rule.

### Gate E

```sh
dpkg -V calango-desktop   # no output -- every shipped file matches its checksum
```

`dpkg -V` exits 0 whether or not it finds anything, so the gate is the
OUTPUT, not the exit status: a clean install prints nothing at all, and any
line of output names a shipped file -- the greetd session entry or
`/etc/default/slack` among them -- that has since been hand-edited or has
drifted from the package. `home/bootstrap.nix`'s own activation hook runs
this same command at every switch and reports exactly this asymmetry.
